────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  All three experimental arms were confirmed in iteration 1. The hierarchical search strategy found 82.9% of Pareto-optimal configurations using only ~200 out of 1800 possible evaluations with 96.4% precision, both exceeding thresholds. Tensor parallelism (TP) dominance and KV cache degradation behavior matched predictions, with one notable surprise about the cache plateau threshold.

  * h-main (search efficiency): CONFIRMED — 82.9% recall (≥80% required) and 96.4% precision (≥95% required) using ~200/1800 evaluations. The search correctly exploited TP dominance, cache equivalence, and maxr plateau to prune the space by ~9x. Six missed Pareto tuples were at intermediate kv values (5000, 7500) not prioritized by the search.
  * h-dose-response (TP scaling): CONFIRMED — At 8 GPUs, increasing TP from 1→8 (while decreasing NI from 8→1) yielded monotone increasing throughput (11.93→32.01 rps, a 2.68x gain) and monotone decreasing TTFT p99 (47.4→27.5 ms). Diminishing returns observed at tp=8: marginal gain dropped from ~7.4 rps per GPU-doubling to ~5.2 rps.
  * h-tradeoff (KV cache degradation): CONFIRMED — Maximum throughput degradation was only 24.4% at kv=2000 (well below the 50% ceiling prediction). Preemption-based degradation is gradual: kv=3000 shows 14.7% loss with 97 preemptions, kv=4000 only 0.9% loss.
  * Unexpected finding: The KV cache plateau (zero preemptions, no performance loss) extends down to kv=5000 with bs=32 (160K effective tokens), rather than the estimated kv=3000/bs=32 (96K tokens). This suggests the simulated workload's actual KV footprint is larger than the prefix_tokens=512 parameter implied, which is worth noting for future simulation configurations.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

