────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether adding an adaptive 'degraded-regime interaction' phase to the search algorithm—specifically exploring SJF scheduler × low concurrency (64, 128) combinations in cache-constrained configurations—can raise unique Pareto tuple recall from 82.9% (iter-1 baseline) to ≥90% within a 200-evaluation budget. A secondary robustness arm checks whether the improved search generalizes to arrival rates of 50 and 200 (beyond the primary rate of 100).

  * h-main: After the existing Phase 2 cache sweep in search.py, a new Phase 2.5 identifies configs where rps < 95% of ceiling (indicating cache degradation), then tests SJF × {maxr=64, maxr=128} at those kv/bs values. This directly targets the 6 Pareto tuples missed in iter-1, all of which involved SJF and/or low maxr in degraded cache regimes. Success criterion: ≥32/35 unique Pareto tuples recalled (≥90%) with ≥95% precision, ≤200 total evaluations.
  * h-robustness: The same improved search is run at rate=50 and rate=200 against their respective full-enumeration Pareto fronts. Success criterion: ≥80% recall at both rates. Failure would indicate the search's structural assumptions (TP dominance, cache equivalence, maxr plateau) break down under different load levels.
  * Confirmation: recall ≥90% at rate=100 confirms the mechanism that scheduler × concurrency interactions in cache-degraded regimes produce invisible Pareto tradeoffs. Refutation: if recall stays near 83%, diagnostics check whether the 95% ceiling threshold misidentifies degraded configs, whether missed tuples involve non-SJF interactions, or whether third-order knob interactions are needed. Each BLIS evaluation takes ~0.06s, so the full experiment (search + enumeration at 3 rates) should complete in under 10 minutes.

