  Iteration 5 tests whether restructuring the BLIS search algorithm — by making Phase 2 selective (skip redundant KV sweeps for non-degrading combos), eliminating Phase 3 entirely (bs=16 never Pareto-optimal), and expanding Phase 2.5 to probe non-best combos and near-ceiling SJF configs — can raise unique Pareto tuple recall from 80.0% to ≥95% at budget=100 on the 14B/rate=100 workload.

  * Three code changes to search.py: (1) Phase 2 only extends to kv=4000,5000 for combos still degraded at kv=3000, saving ~22 evals; (2) Phase 3 (bs=16 sweep, 30 evals) removed entirely since bs=32 dominates on all objectives; (3) Phase 2.5 expanded to test non-best combos (e.g., (1,2), (4,2)) with sjf+128/sjf+64/fcfs+64 and near-ceiling SJF probes at first non-degraded kv.
  * Main hypothesis predicts ≥95% recall at budget=100 (up from 80.0% baseline). Dose-response arm runs budgets [50, 75, 100, 125, 150, 200] expecting a saturating curve that hits 100% by budget=100, eliminating the dead zone seen in v1 between budget 100–125. Negative control confirms baseline still yields exactly 80.0% recall.
  * Confirmation criteria: ≥95% recall at budget=100 for main arm; monotone saturating dose-response curve reaching 100% no later than budget=100; baseline unchanged at 80.0%. Refutation diagnostics specify checking whether missed tuples come from incorrect degradation thresholds (0.99), wrong kv selection rules, or priority ordering issues in Phase 2.5.

